    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A care plan is only as good as its last honest review. Needs change. Choices change. The paper does not keep up. An audit catches the drift. The hard part is doing it without copying private care data into yet another system.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>You sample plans. Are they current, person-centred and consented?</li><li>Inspectors sample plans and ask families. A stale plan is a common Fault.</li><li>The rules differ in England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. The core test is the same.</li><li>Consent must be shown. If capacity is in doubt, so must the right assessment.</li><li>The audit does not change the plan. The keyworker does.</li><li>Quickler records your finding, not the plan. No care data is copied.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What good looks like",
      "heading": "Current, person-centred and consented",
      "html": "<p>Current: reviewed on time and after any big change. Person-centred: written around the person, their history and wishes, not a template with the name changed. Consented: there is proof the person agreed. If capacity is in doubt, the right assessment was done and recorded.</p><p>You do not need to read every plan. A good sample, rotated over time, tells you enough. Use your regulator's latest guidance. This page is general information, not clinical or legal advice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Private data",
      "heading": "Audit plans without moving the data",
      "html": "<p>A care plan is very private. An audit app that copies it spreads that data into a second system. That is a risk, just to record a yes or no.</p><p>Quickler works the other way. You read the plan in your care system. You record only the finding: reviewed, current, consented, or not, and the review job. No care data crosses over. Your care system stays the one source of truth.</p>"
    },
